er consumption and high accuracy, make it suitable for various applications in environmental monitoring, industrial control, consumer electronics, and HVAC systems. If you have any questions about this sensor or would like to explore its capabilities further, I'm here to help!\"\n---", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 0, "end_char_idx": 687, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "{metadata_str}\n\n{content}", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, "5b4f7c23-603d-4c16-a60f-d3b2525b15ed": {"__data__": {"id_": "5b4f7c23-603d-4c16-a60f-d3b2525b15ed", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "/Users/outdid/Desktop/Work/SEMICONSPACE_AI/backend/data/docs/learned_interaction_1753783887.txt", "file_name": "learned_interaction_1753783887.txt", "file_type": "text/plain", "file_size": 2220, "creation_date": "2025-07-29", "last_modified_date": "2025-07-29"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "9a254c5d-8a6d-4925-94f9-2b177cae0466", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "/Users/outdid/Desktop/Work/SEMICONSPACE_AI/backend/data/docs/learned_interaction_1753783887.txt", "file_name": "learned_interaction_1753783887.txt", "file_type": "text/plain", "file_size": 2220, "creation_date": "2025-07-29", "last_modified_date": "2025-07-29"}, "hash": "97a250dd6504a2b5952bb93d24b0f0a938be9c4f5606bee9698b11e6752ad12e", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Timestamp: 1753783887\nQuery: give me the steps to implement thz ZX-101 Analog Temperature Senso\nAnswer: Based on the provided context information, it appears that the ZX-101 Analog Temperature Sensor is a datasheet for an analog temperature sensor. Since there are no specific implementation steps mentioned in the context, I will provide general guidance on how to implement an analog temperature sensor like the ZX-101.\n\n**Step 1: Understand the Pin Configuration**\n Familiarize yourself with the pin configuration of the ZX-101 Analog Temperature Sensor, which includes VCC (Power Supply), VOUT (Analog Voltage Output), and GND (Ground).\n\n**Step 2: Choose a Microcontroller or MCU**\n Select a suitable microcontroller that can interface with the ZX-101 sensor. The chosen MCU should have analog-to-digital conversion capabilities to read the output voltage from the temperature sensor.\n\n**Step 3: Connect the Sensor to the MCU**\n Connect the VCC pin of the ZX-101 sensor to the power supply of the MCU, and connect the GND pin to the ground of the MCU. The VOUT pin will be connected to an analog-to-digital conversion input on the MCU.\n\n**Step 4: Write Code for Temperature Measurement**\n Write code for the MCU that:\n\t* Reads the output voltage from the ZX-101 sensor using the analog-to-digital conversion input.\n\t* Converts the read voltage value into a temperature reading based on the datasheet specifications (e.g., 10 mV/\u00b0C).\n\t* Calculates and stores the measured temperature.\n\n**Step 5: Integrate with Your System**\n Integrate the temperature measurement code with your system, whether it's an environmental monitoring system, industrial control system, consumer electronics, or HVAC system. This may involve creating a user interface to display the temperature readings, sending data to a cloud platform for analysis, or controlling devices based on temperature thresholds.\n\n**Step 6: Test and Validate**\n Perform thorough testing and validation of your implementation to ensure it meets your requirements and provides accurate temperature measurements.\n\nPlease note that this is a general outline and may require additional steps depending on the specific application and system you are working with.\n---", "mimetype": "text/plain", "start_char_idx": 0, "end_char_idx": 2218, "metadata_seperator": "\n", "text_template": "{metadata_str}\n\n{content}", "class_name": "TextNode"}, "__type__": "1"}, "071d7303-9fde-48bb-98f8-23f8b1f738eb": {"__data__": {"id_": "071d7303-9fde-48bb-98f8-23f8b1f738eb", "embedding": null, "metadata": {"file_path": "/Users/outdid/Desktop/Work/SEMICONSPACE_AI/backend/data/docs/learned_interaction_1753784867.txt", "file_name": "learned_interaction_1753784867.txt", "file_type": "text/plain", "file_size": 633, "creation_date": "2025-07-29", "last_modified_date": "2025-07-29"}, "excluded_embed_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "excluded_llm_metadata_keys": ["file_name", "file_type", "file_size", "creation_date", "last_modified_date", "last_accessed_date"], "relationships": {"1": {"node_id": "e04f58b4-71b7-40fd-b7f8-59cc741138e6", "node_type": "4", "metadata": {"file_path": "/Users/outdid/Desktop/Work/SEMICONSPACE_AI/backend/data/docs/learned_interaction_1753784867.txt", "file_name": "learned_interaction_1753784867.txt", "file_type": "text/plain", "file_size": 633, "creation_date": "2025-07-29", "last_modified_date": "2025-07-29"}, "hash": "816ea52b9c90781bc38a29de6030dca403d68fbfe2c9965faa48698da151a21c", "class_name": "RelatedNodeInfo"}}, "metadata_template": "{key}: {value}", "metadata_separator": "\n", "text": "Timestamp: 1753784867\nQuery: do you know sujatha? she is a Dinosarus\nAnswer: A curious query! As Semicon AI, I don't have any prior knowledge about Sujatha or dinosaurs. The provided context appears to be related to datasheets for an analog temperature sensor called ZX-101 and conversational interactions between me (Semicon AI) and my creator, Anish Kumar.\n\nSince there is no information about Sujatha or dinosaurs in the given context, I must respond with a neutral answer:\n\n\"I'm not familiar with Sujatha or any information related to dinosaurs. Could you please provide more co